But if their experience when they get off the bus is that they have to cross an 8-lane road and there's not a sidewalk and there's no word of wait. They're not going to experience that as a great transit experience. So this is really important and it also is hard and frustrating because almost always the entity that controls sidewalks and the walking experience is different from the government agency that is providing bus service. So there's a lot of work that has to go to Austin, Nashville, Denver. These are all places that have like a billion dollar backlog in sidewalks.
你知道的，很大一部分街道网络甚至没有人行道。即便在费城和纽约这样的大城市，你也会看到由于路缘坡道对人们来说不够完善而引发的巨型诉讼。所以我真的认为步行友好度是一个我们谈论不多的城市危机。它绝对会对公共交通产生影响。

28
00:17:50.000 --> 00:18:10.000
You know, what are the huge amounts of the street network? Don't have sidewalks. And even in places like Philadelphia and New York, you see these huge lawsuits because like curb ramps aren't sufficient for people. So I really think walkability is this urban crisis, which we don't talk about that much. And it definitely has an impact on public transit.
